From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nate Isern <nate@oxy.so>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Classify itertools as shippable, because Astro ships it

Astro links Brave's `adblock` engine into the browser process, and adblock
0.9.8 depends on `itertools`. Chromium declares `group = 'test'` for itertools
in `gnrt_config.toml`, so gnrt writes `testonly = true` into the generated
`//third_party/rust/itertools/v0_13:lib`, privilege flows down to
`//third_party/rust/adblock/v0_9:lib`, and
`//chrome/browser/oxy/adblock/rs:adblock_engine_ffi_cxx_generated`, which is
not testonly, may not depend on it. That is a hard `gn gen` failure and it is
the last one between a reset checkout and a complete build graph.

WHY THE OVERRIDE CANNOT GO ON adblock

`find_inherited_privilege_group` (tools/crates/gnrt/lib/inherit.rs) computes

    ancestor_privilege   = fold(ancestor_groups,   Test, max)
    dependency_privilege = fold(dependency_groups, Safe, min)
    privilege            = min(ancestor_privilege, dependency_privilege)

and a group declared on the crate itself replaces only the ancestor list. So
`[crate.adblock] group = 'safe'` raises the side that is already Safe and
leaves the dependency side at Test. The classification has to move on the
dependency that carries it, which is itertools.

WHAT 'test' ACTUALLY RECORDS

`gnrt_config.toml` documents `group` as "where the library is allowed to be
used and where its GN rules will be written" -- a statement about Chromium's
own usage, not a verdict on the crate. Nothing in the tree records an audit
rejecting itertools; it reaches the build only through prost-derive, which
Chromium uses in tests. In the generated rule `testonly` is literally
`dep.group == Group::Test` (tools/crates/gnrt/lib/gn.rs).

THE AUDIT, BECAUSE 'safe' IS A CLAIM ABOUT SOURCE

itertools 0.13.0 is an iterator-adaptor library, and it is not unsafe-free.
`grep -rn unsafe third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/vendor/itertools-v0_13/src`
returns 5 lines, all in one file, `src/either_or_both.rs`, in three public
methods of the `EitherOrBoth` enum:

  * `insert_left` (line 393) and `insert_right` (435): a move in place -- a
    `ptr::read` of the current payload immediately followed by a `ptr::write`
    of `Both(..)` over `*self`. No user code runs between the two, so there is
    no panic window in which the payload exists twice.
  * `insert_left` (405), `insert_right` (446), `insert_both` (461):
    `hint::unreachable_unchecked()` on the else arm of an `if let Both(..)`
    re-match, three lines after the code assigns `Both(..)` to `*self`.

No transmutes, no pointer arithmetic, no unchecked indexing, no FFI, no
`unsafe impl Send`/`Sync`. itertools 0.11.0 carries the same five, unchanged.

Reachability is recorded rather than relied on: adblock names itertools in
five places -- the `Itertools` trait, `Either`, and `itertools::join` -- and
never `EitherOrBoth` nor any of the three methods, and nothing else inside
itertools calls them either, so the generic functions holding every unsafe
block are never instantiated. 'safe' is still a claim about the whole crate,
and the audit above is what backs it.

WHY THE EDIT IS NOT EPOCH-SCOPED

Two epochs are vendored: 0.13 for adblock, 0.11 for prost-derive. gnrt does
support `[crate."itertools@v0_13"]`, and scoping the change that way would
leave prost-derive exactly as upstream has it. It cannot be used here.
`fill_allow_unsafe_settings` (tools/crates/gnrt/vendor.rs) keys its edits on
the bare package name -- with a TODO, crbug.com/419104870, naming that gap --
so the next `gnrt vendor` writes a bare `[crate.itertools]` beside the
versioned ones, and `BuildConfig::validate` then rejects the whole file:
mixing versioned and unversioned config for one crate is an error. The
unversioned edit is the one the tooling supports.

Its cost, measured rather than reasoned about: exactly one crate moves that
did not have to. Regenerating the tree with this line changed removes
`testonly` from three BUILD.gn files -- adblock/v0_9, itertools/v0_13 (both
intended) and itertools/v0_11 (the collateral) -- and flips
itertools/v0_11/README.chromium from `Shipped: no` / `Security Critical: no`
to yes. Nothing else in third_party/rust changes. prost and prost-derive, the
only consumers of 0.11, are unaffected: they declare `group = 'test'` on
themselves, so their own classification does not depend on itertools'. No
target gains a dependency it did not have; what changes is a permission and a
record.

WHY 'safe' AND NOT 'sandbox'

Both clear the build -- the generated rule marks `testonly` only for
`Group::Test`. But the sandbox group's visibility restriction is an
unimplemented TODO in tools/crates/gnrt/lib/gn.rs, so 'sandbox' would be a
restriction nothing enforces, and a false one besides: AstroAdBlockService is
a `KeyedService` and the engine runs in the browser process, which is not
sandboxed. 'safe' is the accurate statement.

WHERE THIS SITS IN THE PIPELINE

Before vendoring, which is the existing order (sync-sources, apply-patches,
vendor-adblock-rust, sync-overlay, build). gnrt reads `gnrt_config.toml` and
writes the `BUILD.gn` files that carry the classification, so the line has to
be in the file before gnrt runs.

MEASURED

Patch applied, `tools/vendor-adblock-rust.sh` re-run so gnrt rewrote the
generated build files, then from chromium/src with the committed
gn_args/linux.gn (verified byte-identical to out/GateCheck/args.gn, so the
configuration is the repository's and not a local edit):

    $ ./buildtools/linux64/gn gen out/GateCheck
    Done. Made 29928 targets from 4409 files in 3104ms
    exit 0

Exit 0 is also the no-cycle result: gn reports a dependency cycle as a hard
error. With `group = 'test'` the same tree fails, which is what out/GateCheck
held before this patch:

    //chrome/browser/oxy/adblock/rs:adblock_engine_ffi_cxx_generated
    which is NOT marked testonly can't depend on
    //third_party/rust/adblock/v0_9:lib which is marked testonly.

The target count is the vacuity floor. A `gn gen` that fails writes no build
graph at all, and every check downstream of one reads clean against nothing;
29,928 targets from 4,409 files is the evidence that something was measured.

diff --git a/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml b/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
--- a/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
+++ b/third_party/rust/chromium_crates_io/gnrt_config.toml
@@ -408,7 +408,12 @@ allow_unsafe = true
 
 [crate.itertools]
 extra_kv = { allow_unsafe = true }
-group = 'test'
+# Astro: `adblock` depends on `itertools`, and Astro links the ad-blocking
+# engine into the browser process, so the generated target cannot be testonly.
+# Upstream's 'test' records that Chromium itself only used the crate in tests;
+# it is not an audit verdict against shipping it. The unsafe audit backing this
+# line is in the Astro patch that sets it.
+group = 'safe'
 
 [crate.itoa.extra_kv]
 allow_unsafe = true
